WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.380
SPEAKER: So this
demo is about showing

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.720
how Kubeflow and
Kubernetics together

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.680
can make machine learning really
easy, scalable, and portable.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.140
So this demo is about the
operational efficiency

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.940
that you gain by adopting
Kubernetics and Kubeflow.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.330
So the sample use case
we have taken here

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:24.410
is that of predicting sentiments
associated with reviews.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.779
And so here's a very
simple dev app, right?

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:29.820
Let's go over some of the
reviews that are sample

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.440
reviews that are listed here.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.695
First one says, "It's
a typical McDonald's.

00:00:33.695 --> 00:00:35.210
Food was typical.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.210
Employees were typical."

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.460
Everything is typical.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:42.050
So there's really nothing
positive about this review.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.390
And so we would expect it
to be a negative review.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.870
You don't want to highlight
it on your restaurant website

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.670
as one of the premier reviews.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.015
And the second one
here, it says it's good.

00:00:53.015 --> 00:00:54.130
"The service is good.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.390
Overall, they do a good job."

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.150
So this seems to be a
pretty positive review,

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.941
so we should probably
be highlighting that.

00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:01.910
Whereas on the
right side here, it

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.670
says, "Really excited to hear
of this restaurant coming

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.090
to Toronto," and
so on and so on.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.060
"The service here is not great."

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.670
So it's not a review that
you want to highlight.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.560
So it's probably
a negative review.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.100
So what would you expect--

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.850
let's say you throw this
task at your engineer,

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.370
and then your
engineer just builds

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.530
something, some back-end
system, to classify

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.070
them as positive or negative.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.940
Let's see what the
engineers built.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.250
Turns out, what the
engineer has built doesn't

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.400
meet what the humans expect.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.310
It's literally classifying
them incorrectly.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:38.250
Let's go look at the code
that the engineer has used.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.346
This is not good code.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.660
It's really dumb code.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.390
I don't know how
that got checked in.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.820
But in any case, it's there.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.210
So now let's go improve
it and let's go fix it.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.750
So this is where machine
learning comes to the rescue.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.620
There's quite a few
machine learning models

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.370
that you can use to solve these
sort of language problems.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.740
And Tensor2Tensor happens to be
one really popular model that

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.280
can be used for lots of
different applications

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.210
like language modeling,
sentiment analysis,

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.669
summarization, and so on.

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:13.290
And so sentiment analysis is
one application for this model.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:14.880
So what we're
going to do now is,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.320
we're going to use Kubeflow.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.245
We're going to use this
Tensor2Tensor preexisting model

00:02:19.245 --> 00:02:20.870
and we're going to
operationalize that.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.410
We're going to train that
model using public data that's

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.360
provided by year.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.050
And then what we're
going to do is,

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.777
we're going to deploy that
model to a Kubernetics cluster

00:02:32.777 --> 00:02:34.360
and then see if I
can improve this web

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:39.374
app to become smarter and make
the right kind of predictions.

00:02:39.374 --> 00:02:41.540
So at this point, I'm going
to switch to my terminal

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.860
here where I'm going
to run the demo.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.110
So the first stage of
the demo is that I'm

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.400
going to switch to Minikube.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.774
Then what I'm
going to do is, I'm

00:02:48.774 --> 00:02:53.460
going to deploy Kubeflow to
my local Minikube cluster.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.590
Kubeflow prepackaged
with a few components.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.780
I'm going to show you
what components exist.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.695
And you're also going to
see that Kubeflow extracts

00:03:01.695 --> 00:03:03.990
out a lot of different
Kubernetics constructs

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.660
that you have to put together
in order to build applications

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.075
that data scientists and ML
practitioners can consume.

00:03:10.075 --> 00:03:12.450
They find it really hard to
consume Kubernetics directly.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:17.700
So Kubeflow acts as the glue
between the apps that data

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.700
scientists like and the
infrastructure efficiency

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.440
that Kubernetics provides you.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.982
So I'm switching to my
local Minikube context here.

00:03:26.982 --> 00:03:28.440
The next thing that
I'm going to do

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.490
is, I'm going to deploy
Kubeflow locally.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.670
So it's as simple as
running one simple command.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.130
Then the next
thing is, I'm going

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.990
to show Jupyter Notebooks.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:40.110
So this is part of Kubeflow,
that you have Jupyter Notebooks

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.250
that are built in.

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:47.130
So you can go in and log in
and create your own Notebooks.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.970
There are many
pre-canned images.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:51.810
And you can choose
different CPU.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.942
You can choose different memory.

00:03:53.942 --> 00:03:56.400
And then you can also choose
GPUs if you happen to have it.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.900
But Minikube doesn't
support it yet,

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.870
so you probably can't
use GPUs locally.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.560
But you get a pretty good
set of configurations,

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.350
and you can bring
your own images.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:08.970
So what this provides is, it
provides you a local Notebook

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.820
environment through which you
can iterate locally and build

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.980
models interactively, locally.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.350
But we're not going
to use Notebooks now.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.839
Instead, what we're going to
do is, we're going to go back

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:20.970
and we're going to
continue training

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.229
our model using command line.

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.020
And specifically what
we're going to do is,

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.600
we're going to use a
Tf job operator that's

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.500
packaged as part of Kubeflow.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.140
The Tf job operator lets
you do distributed training

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.240
and single-load training in
a really easy, portable way.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.870
So similar to how you
define a deployment or a job

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:45.630
in Kubernetics, you can define a
TensorFlow job using the Tf job

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.506
operator.

00:04:46.506 --> 00:04:47.880
And then the
TensorFlow job, what

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.790
it does is takes your
TensorFlow application

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.920
package as containers
and sets up

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.070
the runtime environment that
TensorFlow needs in order

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.950
to make it work in a distributed
setting and in a local setting,

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.730
and extracts all of that.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.440
So I'm going to
configure my TensorFlow

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.240
model to run locally.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.700
I've set the data to be
available as locally,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.570
and I've set local
training to be true.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.050
And the model is now deployed.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.310
I'm going to check if the
training jobs are running.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:15.580
They seem to be running.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.490
Then the next thing
is I'm going to check

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.600
if the model is actually
running by looking at the logs.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.860
OK, looks like the
model is running.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.960
But I'm going to stop
here with local training,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.980
because it's going
to take a long time.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:29.580
It's probably going to
take a couple of days

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.320
for local training to complete.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.870
But the point of
training locally

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:38.040
is not to build a model, but
it's rather to just make sure

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:39.250
that my model works.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.830
As soon as I know
my model works,

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.210
I have the cloud to the rescue.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.305
Now I can switch to the cloud
and use elastic compute data.

00:05:45.305 --> 00:05:46.930
And that's what we're
going to do next.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.730
And you're going to use the same
Kubeflow framework to do that.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:51.180
So I'm going to switch
to a GK cluster.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.846
And I'm going to show
you the GK cluster

00:05:52.846 --> 00:05:54.780
that I have pre-provisioned.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.550
So here's a GK cluster.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:00.930
This cluster has GPUs and
TPUs already attached.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.670
So I pre-provisioned that.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:06.960
It's very easy to provision GK
clusters with TPUs and GPUs.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.840
It's literally
running two commands.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.240
And you get late drivers and
everything pre-installed.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.160
So I have GPUs attached here.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.070
And TPUs are available
from all nodes.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:20.820
And I also pre-provisioned
a TPU here.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.794
And the TPU is running
TensorFlow version 1.7.

00:06:24.794 --> 00:06:26.377
So the next thing
that I'm going to do

00:06:26.377 --> 00:06:30.150
is, I'm going to go
back to my terminal.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:34.370
I'm going to switch context
and switch to this GK cluster.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.232
And I'm going to
deploy Kubeflow to GKE.

00:06:37.232 --> 00:06:38.940
And once I've deployed
Kubeflow, then I'm

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:43.370
going to deploy my model to GKE.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:48.610
So here we're deploying
Kubeflow to GKE.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:53.300
The next step is, let
me deploy the model.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.522
So I have to change the
model parameters such

00:06:55.522 --> 00:06:56.730
that it can run in the cloud.

00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:01.780
I've enabled GPUs for the
cloud, and also enabled

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.580
a set of data that are
created to be on GCS.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:07.120
And I have also
disabled local training.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.510
So once the model is deployed--

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:11.620
next off, in
addition to GPUs, you

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:17.120
can also use TPUs
in Google Cloud.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.290
And now I'm going to
deploy the same model,

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.330
but this time with TPUs.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.910
So here you go.

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:29.780
I've configured TPUs, a few
parameters to make the model

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:30.680
point at a TPU.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.300
And then the models are now
deployed on TPUs as well.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.480
Now we're going to wait
for the pods to be running.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.990
So I'm going to
switch to the GK UI

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.690
and I'm going to filter
on my demo namespace.

00:07:44.967 --> 00:07:46.550
And once I've filtered
that, I'm going

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.565
to check whether my
training pods are running.

00:07:48.565 --> 00:07:50.490
So turns out, my training
pods are running.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.990
You see here that you have a
distributor cluster running.

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:56.670
So you have two workers and a
master and a parameter server.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:57.990
These are TensorFlow concepts.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.950
Don't worry about it
if you don't know it.

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:03.460
And there's also a TPU
training job that's running.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:05.070
So if I go into the
TPU training job--

00:08:08.792 --> 00:08:10.250
if we go into the
TPU training job,

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.970
then you would know that the
TPU training job is running.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:15.610
And then there's
the GPU job here.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.250
And then the GPU job is running.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.510
And you can see here the GPU
memory and GPU unitcycle are

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:22.434
also being shown here for you.

00:08:22.434 --> 00:08:24.350
And the pod is running,
and you can go in here

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.090
and you can see the logs.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:28.550
The training is actually
going on already,

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:31.120
and it's saving
checkpoints and so on.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.720
So once we go back, then--

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:36.549
OK, so training is
going on, but training

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:38.470
is going to take quite a while.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:40.480
What can we do meanwhile?

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.520
So in addition to that,
we also get Tensible.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.132
I forgot to mention this.

00:08:46.132 --> 00:08:46.840
You get Tensible.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:50.950
That's going to help you track
the progress of your training

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:53.390
both locally and in the cloud.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:58.037
What I've done here is, I'm
showing you Tensible graphs

00:08:58.037 --> 00:09:00.370
based on pre-trained models
that I ran through training.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:01.661
And this is what you would see.

00:09:01.661 --> 00:09:03.370
So what you see here
is, for GPUs, you

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:06.580
see a training rate of
about three, roughly.

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:09.460
Like 2.5 steps per second.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.160
And then in the case of
TPUs, if you scroll down,

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.590
you see a much
higher learning rate.

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:18.850
And so this is to give
you a rough idea of what

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.920
sort of hardware you might use.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.750
And in any case,
Kubernetics and Kubeflow

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.780
can work with both of them.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:30.020
So now that we have gone
through the training process

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:34.096
and now that training is
done, the next office--

00:09:34.096 --> 00:09:37.626
we can't wait for
training to complete.

00:09:37.626 --> 00:09:39.000
And so what we're
going to do now

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.580
is we're going to take
a pre-trained model

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.340
and deploy it to
the same GK cluster.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:49.530
And then we're going to see
if our restaurant sentiment

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.360
analysis UI can perform better.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.570
So I'm going to go ahead and
deploy a pre-trained model

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:56.760
locally--

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.860
I said, sorry,
into my GK cluster.

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:00.690
And once that model
is deployed, now I

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:03.690
can go back and see if
the model is running.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.450
So I'm going to refresh.

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:11.580
And my model is running.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.060
The pods are running.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:17.210
So let's switch back to my UI.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.390
So I have the UI here.

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:23.330
But local classification,
it did not

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:25.192
do well, as we would expected.

00:10:25.192 --> 00:10:27.650
So now, let's see if it does
well with the machine learning

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:28.550
model.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.600
Yay, it does well.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:36.060
It's classifying the first
and the third review correctly

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.480
as being negative, and it's
classifying the second review

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:39.960
as being positive.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:43.890
So this demo should
have shown you

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:48.050
how the overall pipeline
for machine learning

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:50.840
can be simplified with
Kubeflow and Kubernetics

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.090
together, where your
infrastructure concerns

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:55.030
and application
packaging concerns are

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:56.690
taken care of for you.

00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:00.110
And you focus on building great
machine learning packages that

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:03.920
are reusable and making a plain
machine learning really, really

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:06.820
easy for the rest of the world.

